Here is the complete, robust Java function. It is designed to handle all the edge cases you mentioned (empty arrays, arrays with one element, arrays with all equal values) as well as potential integer overflow issues. 

It returns `Integer` (the wrapper class) instead of `int` so it can safely return `null` when a second-largest number doesn't exist, which prevents ambiguity with `Integer.MIN_VALUE`.

```java
public class Solution {

    public static Integer findSecondLargest(int[] arr) {
        // Edge case: null, empty, or single-element array
        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
            return null; 
        }

        Integer largest = null;
        Integer secondLargest = null;

        for (int num : arr) {
            if (largest == null || num > largest) {
                // New largest found; shift current largest to second largest
                secondLargest = largest;
                largest = num;
            } else if (num < largest && (secondLargest == null || num > secondLargest)) {
                // num is smaller than largest but larger than current second largest
                secondLargest = num;
            }
        }

        // Note: If all elements in the array are equal, secondLargest remains null
        return secondLargest; 
    }

    // Optional: Main method for quick local testing
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(findSecondLargest(new int[]{5, 5, 5, 5})); // null
        System.out.println(findSecondLargest(new int[]{}));           // null
        System.out.println(findSecondLargest(new int[]{3, 1, 2}));    // 2
        System.out.println(findSecondLargest(new int[]{5, 5, 4}));    // 4
        System.out.println(findSecondLargest(new int[]{-1, -2, -3})); // -2
    }
}
```

**Why this passes edge cases:**
1. **Empty/Short Arrays:** The `arr.length < 2` check immediately catches empty or single-element arrays.
2. **All Equal Values:** Because the `else if` strictly checks `num < largest`, equal values are completely ignored. If all values are equal, `secondLargest` stays `null`.
3. **Negative Numbers:** Initializing as `null` instead of `0` or `Integer.MIN_VALUE` ensures it correctly handles arrays with all negative numbers.

If your auto-grader **strictly requires** a primitive `int` return type and throws an exception instead of returning null on edge cases, use this alternate version:

